      "updated": 1784390058975,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "RSS\n\n2. How rss_stat Maps Under the Hood\n\nThe NR_MM_COUNTERS array slot indexes still track the same core residency metrics:\n\n    MM_FILEPAGES: File-backed memory pages.\n\n    MM_ANONPAGES: Anonymous memory pages (what you knew as anon_rss).\n\n    MM_SWAPENTS: Pages that have been swapped out to disk.\n\n    MM_SHMEMPAGES: Shared memory pages (IPC, shmget, anonymous MAP_SHARED).\n\nBy changing this array to a per-CPU counter structure, a thread running on Core 0 increments Core 0's private count bucket. A thread on Core 128 increments Core 128's private bucket. No cross-talk, zero locks, and maximum execution speed on the hot allocation path.\n\n3. Reading the Value is No Longer Free\n\nBecause the counter values are scattered across individual per-core structures, you can no longer read a simple member scalar from GDB or C code.\n\nThe kernel's wrapper macro get_mm_counter(mm, member) now has to sum up the data across the core matrix:\nC\n\nstatic inline unsigned long get_mm_counter(struct mm_struct *mm, int member)\n{\n    // Sums up the local counters across all CPUs, ensuring it doesn't return a negative fluke\n    return percpu_counter_sum_positive(&mm->rss_stat[member]);\n}\n\n(Note: In the newest hierarchical tree variants, this calls percpu_counter_tree_precise_sum_positive to aggregate data faster across NUMA nodes.)",
      "fontSize": 36,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "RSS\n\n2. How rss_stat Maps Under the Hood\n\nThe NR_MM_COUNTERS array slot indexes still track the same core residency metrics:\n\n    MM_FILEPAGES: File-backed memory pages.\n\n    MM_ANONPAGES: Anonymous memory pages (what you knew as anon_rss).\n\n    MM_SWAPENTS: Pages that have been swapped out to disk.\n\n    MM_SHMEMPAGES: Shared memory pages (IPC, shmget, anonymous MAP_SHARED).\n\nBy changing this array to a per-CPU counter structure, a thread running on Core 0 increments Core 0's private count bucket. A thread on Core 128 increments Core 128's private bucket. No cross-talk, zero locks, and maximum execution speed on the hot allocation path.\n\n3. Reading the Value is No Longer Free\n\nBecause the counter values are scattered across individual per-core structures, you can no longer read a simple member scalar from GDB or C code.\n\nThe kernel's wrapper macro get_mm_counter(mm, member) now has to sum up the data across the core matrix:\nC\n\nstatic inline unsigned long get_mm_counter(struct mm_struct *mm, int member)\n{\n    // Sums up the local counters across all CPUs, ensuring it doesn't return a negative fluke\n    return percpu_counter_sum_positive(&mm->rss_stat[member]);\n}\n\n(Note: In the newest hierarchical tree variants, this calls percpu_counter_tree_precise_sum_positive to aggregate data faster across NUMA nodes.)",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "created": null,
      "labelPosition": null,
      "baseFontSize": null
    },
