      "updated": 1784390317978,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "pinned_vm\n\nThis tracks pages that have been locked into place using specialized kernel routines like pin_user_pages(). \nUnlike locked_vm (which is triggered by userspace calling mlock() to prevent swapping), pinned_vm is used by \nhigh-performance kernel subsystems—like RDMA (Remote Direct Memory Access) or Direct I/O.\n\nWhen a page is pinned, the kernel cannot move it at all (even for page migration or defragmentation), because \nan external hardware device is actively reading or writing to that specific physical RAM address. Because multiple \nthreads can pin pages concurrently, this is tracked using an atomic type (atomic64_t) to prevent race conditions \nwithout needing to lock the whole mm_struct.",
      "fontSize": 36,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "pinned_vm\n\nThis tracks pages that have been locked into place using specialized kernel routines like pin_user_pages(). \nUnlike locked_vm (which is triggered by userspace calling mlock() to prevent swapping), pinned_vm is used by \nhigh-performance kernel subsystems—like RDMA (Remote Direct Memory Access) or Direct I/O.\n\nWhen a page is pinned, the kernel cannot move it at all (even for page migration or defragmentation), because \nan external hardware device is actively reading or writing to that specific physical RAM address. Because multiple \nthreads can pin pages concurrently, this is tracked using an atomic type (atomic64_t) to prevent race conditions \nwithout needing to lock the whole mm_struct.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "created": null,
      "labelPosition": null,
      "baseFontSize": null
    },
