      "updated": 1784444395005,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "files_struct {\n    atomic_t count (Simple reference count)\n    unsigned int next_fd (fd for next file to be opened)\n\n    struct fdtable __rcu *fdt \n    struct fdtable fdtab \n    \n    struct file __rcu * fd_array[NR_OPEN_DEFAULT];\n}",
      "fontSize": 36,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "files_struct {\n    atomic_t count (Simple reference count)\n    unsigned int next_fd (fd for next file to be opened)\n\n    struct fdtable __rcu *fdt \n    struct fdtable fdtab \n    \n    struct file __rcu * fd_array[NR_OPEN_DEFAULT];\n}",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "created": null,
      "labelPosition": null,
      "baseFontSize": null
    },
    {
      "id": "hLmDDJJHLw_96VgTy3MI_",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -8339.509137313962,
      "y": -2696.8648800695246,
      "width": 2067.2333984375,
      "height": 540,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#2f9e44",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b08",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1519968945,
      "version": 137,
      "versionNonce": 49247295,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [
        {
          "id": "kj-cWImihUyou7BcgD1AM",
          "type": "arrow"
        }
      ],
      "updated": 1784407399205,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "fdt and fdtable\n\nfdt: This is the pointer the kernel actually uses whenever it looks up a file descriptor \n(using macros like files_fdtable(files)). It points to a struct fdtable which contains the arrays of open files, \nclose-on-exec flags, and bitmasks.\n\nfdtab: This is an embedded, default fallback table.\n\nThe Design Trick: When a process starts, fdt points directly to the internal fdtab right next to it. \nHowever, if your process opens a massive number of files that exceed the default limits, the kernel al\nlocates a brand new, larger struct fdtable on the heap, points fdt to that new heap address, and \nleaves fdtab behind. This prevents a memory allocation penalty for normal processes while remaining infinitely scalable.",
      "fontSize": 36,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "fdt and fdtable\n\nfdt: This is the pointer the kernel actually uses whenever it looks up a file descriptor \n(using macros like files_fdtable(files)). It points to a struct fdtable which contains the arrays of open files, \nclose-on-exec flags, and bitmasks.\n\nfdtab: This is an embedded, default fallback table.\n\nThe Design Trick: When a process starts, fdt points directly to the internal fdtab right next to it. \nHowever, if your process opens a massive number of files that exceed the default limits, the kernel al\nlocates a brand new, larger struct fdtable on the heap, points fdt to that new heap address, and \nleaves fdtab behind. This prevents a memory allocation penalty for normal processes while remaining infinitely scalable.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "created": null,
      "labelPosition": null,
      "baseFontSize": null
    },
    {
      "id": "kj-cWImihUyou7BcgD1AM",
