          "type": "arrow"
        }
      ],
      "updated": 1784448102178,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "f_inode\n\nIn older kernels, the file structure pointed directly to the directory entry cache structure:\n\nTo find the file metadata (the inode) from here, you had to dereference the dentry:\n\n    file -> f_dentry -> d_inode\n\nTo clean up how mount points and directories were tracked, the kernel engineers grouped the \ndentry and the virtual filesystem mount point (vfsmount) into a unified helper structure \ncalled struct path:\n\nDuring this cleanup, f_dentry was removed. To get the dentry, code had to change to:\n\n    file -> f_path.dentry\n\nAnd to get the inode, code had to jump twice:\n\n    file -> f_path.dentry -> d_inode\n\nJumping from the file to the dentry, and then from the dentry to the inode is an \nincredibly frequent operation. It happens on every single read, write, seek, \nand poll system call.\n\nOn modern CPUs, chasing pointers across three different structures located \nin different parts of system RAM often results in CPU L1/L2 cache misses.\n\nTo optimize this hot-path, kernel developers did something simple: they added a \ncached direct pointer right back into struct file:\n\n    struct inode *f_inode;\n\nNow, if the kernel wants to read or write, it doesn't have to look up the dentry first. \nIt immediately dereferences the cached f_inode pointer directly.\n",
      "fontSize": 36,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "f_inode\n\nIn older kernels, the file structure pointed directly to the directory entry cache structure:\n\nTo find the file metadata (the inode) from here, you had to dereference the dentry:\n\n    file -> f_dentry -> d_inode\n\nTo clean up how mount points and directories were tracked, the kernel engineers grouped the \ndentry and the virtual filesystem mount point (vfsmount) into a unified helper structure \ncalled struct path:\n\nDuring this cleanup, f_dentry was removed. To get the dentry, code had to change to:\n\n    file -> f_path.dentry\n\nAnd to get the inode, code had to jump twice:\n\n    file -> f_path.dentry -> d_inode\n\nJumping from the file to the dentry, and then from the dentry to the inode is an \nincredibly frequent operation. It happens on every single read, write, seek, \nand poll system call.\n\nOn modern CPUs, chasing pointers across three different structures located \nin different parts of system RAM often results in CPU L1/L2 cache misses.\n\nTo optimize this hot-path, kernel developers did something simple: they added a \ncached direct pointer right back into struct file:\n\n    struct inode *f_inode;\n\nNow, if the kernel wants to read or write, it doesn't have to look up the dentry first. \nIt immediately dereferences the cached f_inode pointer directly.\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "created": null,
      "labelPosition": null,
      "baseFontSize": null
    },
