      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "i_no\n\n1. The True Identity of a File\n\nIn Linux, file names do not matter to the operating system. File names are purely a user-friendly abstraction managed by the directory cache (struct dentry).\n\nTo the kernel and the file system driver, the actual identity of a file is defined entirely by its inode number (i_ino).\n\n    If two different directory entries (dentry objects) point to two different filenames, but both reference the exact same i_ino on the same filesystem, you have a Hard Link.\n\n    The file's data block mapping, permissions, and size are shared instantly because they both share the exact same struct inode.\n\n2. Bridging the VFS to the Disk Blocks\n\nWhen your application requests data from a file descriptor, the VFS layer hands the operation off to the filesystem driver (like ext4 or btrfs). The driver reads i_ino to compute exactly where that file's metadata structure is located on the physical disk sectors.\n\nFor example, in a traditional ext4 filesystem layout, the disk is divided into \"Block Groups,\" each containing an Inode Table. The driver takes i_ino, calculates its offset within that table, and reads the raw metadata from the disk to populate the rest of the fields inside your struct inode object in RAM.",
      "fontSize": 36,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "i_no\n\n1. The True Identity of a File\n\nIn Linux, file names do not matter to the operating system. File names are purely a user-friendly abstraction managed by the directory cache (struct dentry).\n\nTo the kernel and the file system driver, the actual identity of a file is defined entirely by its inode number (i_ino).\n\n    If two different directory entries (dentry objects) point to two different filenames, but both reference the exact same i_ino on the same filesystem, you have a Hard Link.\n\n    The file's data block mapping, permissions, and size are shared instantly because they both share the exact same struct inode.\n\n2. Bridging the VFS to the Disk Blocks\n\nWhen your application requests data from a file descriptor, the VFS layer hands the operation off to the filesystem driver (like ext4 or btrfs). The driver reads i_ino to compute exactly where that file's metadata structure is located on the physical disk sectors.\n\nFor example, in a traditional ext4 filesystem layout, the disk is divided into \"Block Groups,\" each containing an Inode Table. The driver takes i_ino, calculates its offset within that table, and reads the raw metadata from the disk to populate the rest of the fields inside your struct inode object in RAM.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "created": null,
      "labelPosition": null,
      "baseFontSize": null
    },
