      "updated": 1784466967639,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "1. kuid_t i_uid (Owner User ID)\n\n    What it is: The unique identifier of the user who owns the file.\n\n    The k Prefix Significance: The kuid_t type stands for Kernel User ID. \n    The kernel does not use raw integer UIDs directly here because of modern \n     features like user namespaces (containers). A user might be UID 0 (root) \n     inside an unprivileged LXC/Docker container, but map to UID 100000 on the \n     physical host. kuid_t is a structure abstraction that handles this internal mapping safely.\n\n2. kgid_t i_gid (Owner Group ID)\n\n    What it is: The identifier of the POSIX group assigned to the file.\n\n    The k Prefix Significance: Just like the UID, kgid_t stands for Kernel Group ID. It abstracts \n    the group integer across namespace boundaries to ensure that group permissions are safely \n    translated between host and containers.\n\n3. umode_t i_mode (File Type & Permissions Bitmask)\n\n    What it is: A 16-bit bitmask that stores two critical pieces of data packed together: the file \n    type and the access permissions (what you see when running ls -l).\n\n    The Internal Bit Layout:\n\n        Top 4 bits (File Type): Identifies what kind of inode this is (e.g., standard file, directory, \n         symbolic link, socket, block device). The kernel checks these bits using macros like S_ISREG() or S_ISDIR().\n\n        Bottom 12 bits (Flags & Permissions):\n\n            3 bits for execution flags: SUID (Set User ID), SGID (Set Group ID), and the Sticky Bit.\n\n            9 bits for standard POSIX permissions: User (rwx), Group (rwx), and Others (rwx).\n\nHow the Kernel Evaluates Them Together\n\nWhen a process tries to open a file, the VFS layer calls a helper function (typically generic_permission()) that uses \nthese three exact fields in a strict cascading order:\n\n[ Process attempts to access file ]\n              │\n              ├──► Is Process UID == inode->i_uid? \n              │       YES ──► Check User bits in i_mode\n              │\n              ├──► Is Process GID matching inode->i_gid? \n              │       YES ──► Check Group bits in i_mode\n              │\n              └──► Otherwise ─────────────────────────► Check Other bits in i_mode",
      "fontSize": 36,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "1. kuid_t i_uid (Owner User ID)\n\n    What it is: The unique identifier of the user who owns the file.\n\n    The k Prefix Significance: The kuid_t type stands for Kernel User ID. \n    The kernel does not use raw integer UIDs directly here because of modern \n     features like user namespaces (containers). A user might be UID 0 (root) \n     inside an unprivileged LXC/Docker container, but map to UID 100000 on the \n     physical host. kuid_t is a structure abstraction that handles this internal mapping safely.\n\n2. kgid_t i_gid (Owner Group ID)\n\n    What it is: The identifier of the POSIX group assigned to the file.\n\n    The k Prefix Significance: Just like the UID, kgid_t stands for Kernel Group ID. It abstracts \n    the group integer across namespace boundaries to ensure that group permissions are safely \n    translated between host and containers.\n\n3. umode_t i_mode (File Type & Permissions Bitmask)\n\n    What it is: A 16-bit bitmask that stores two critical pieces of data packed together: the file \n    type and the access permissions (what you see when running ls -l).\n\n    The Internal Bit Layout:\n\n        Top 4 bits (File Type): Identifies what kind of inode this is (e.g., standard file, directory, \n         symbolic link, socket, block device). The kernel checks these bits using macros like S_ISREG() or S_ISDIR().\n\n        Bottom 12 bits (Flags & Permissions):\n\n            3 bits for execution flags: SUID (Set User ID), SGID (Set Group ID), and the Sticky Bit.\n\n            9 bits for standard POSIX permissions: User (rwx), Group (rwx), and Others (rwx).\n\nHow the Kernel Evaluates Them Together\n\nWhen a process tries to open a file, the VFS layer calls a helper function (typically generic_permission()) that uses \nthese three exact fields in a strict cascading order:\n\n[ Process attempts to access file ]\n              │\n              ├──► Is Process UID == inode->i_uid? \n              │       YES ──► Check User bits in i_mode\n              │\n              ├──► Is Process GID matching inode->i_gid? \n              │       YES ──► Check Group bits in i_mode\n              │\n              └──► Otherwise ─────────────────────────► Check Other bits in i_mode",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "created": null,
      "labelPosition": null,
      "baseFontSize": null
    },
