      "updated": 1784469781235,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Hard Links and Symlinks\n\n1. Hard Links: Multiple Names, Same Inode\n\nA hard link is just an additional directory entry (struct dentry) pointing to an existing inode number \n(i_ino) on the same filesystem.\n\n    Under the Hood: When you create a hard link, the kernel allocates a new dentry with the new filename, \n    but it copies the exact same i_ino into it. It also increments the reference counter (i_nlink) inside the struct inode.\n\n    Behavior: Both filenames are completely identical peers. If you delete the original filename, the inode and \n    the file data remain completely intact because i_nlink is still greater than 0. The data is only wiped from the disk when all hard links are deleted.\n\n    Limitations: Because an inode number is only unique within its own filesystem partition, hard links cannot cross filesystem\n     boundaries (e.g., you cannot hard link a file from an ext4 drive onto a btrfs drive). You also cannot hard link directories (to prevent infinite loops in the filesystem graph).\n\n[dentry: file.txt] ─────► i_ino = 142857 ────► [ struct inode ] ──► Data Blocks\n                               ▲\n[dentry: hardlink.txt] ────────┘ (Points directly to the same Inode)\n\n2. Symbolic Links (Symlinks): A Shortcut File\n\nA symlink is a completely distinct, special type of file that contains a text string pointing to the path of another filename.\n\n    Under the Hood: When you create a symlink, the kernel allocates a brand new inode with its own unique i_ino and a \n    distinct struct inode. The file type bits in i_mode are set to S_IFLNK (Symbolic Link). The data blocks of this new i\n    node simply store the text path string of the target file (e.g., \"/home/user/file.txt\").\n\n    Behavior: When a process tries to open a symlink, the VFS layer reads the path string stored inside the symlink's inode, \n    halts the current lookup, and restarts the path resolution algorithm using the target path. If you delete the original \n    target file, the symlink remains but becomes \"broken\" (pointing to a path that no longer exists).\n\n    Advantages: Because it relies entirely on a text path string, symlinks can span across different physical drives and file \n    systems, and they can point directly to directories without restrictions.\n\n[dentry: symlink.txt] ──► i_ino = 999999 ──► [struct inode (S_IFLNK)] ──► Data: \"file.txt\"\n                                                                                │\n                                                                                ▼\n[dentry: file.txt] ─────► i_ino = 142857 ──────────────────────────────► [struct inode] ──► Data Blocks",
      "fontSize": 36,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Hard Links and Symlinks\n\n1. Hard Links: Multiple Names, Same Inode\n\nA hard link is just an additional directory entry (struct dentry) pointing to an existing inode number \n(i_ino) on the same filesystem.\n\n    Under the Hood: When you create a hard link, the kernel allocates a new dentry with the new filename, \n    but it copies the exact same i_ino into it. It also increments the reference counter (i_nlink) inside the struct inode.\n\n    Behavior: Both filenames are completely identical peers. If you delete the original filename, the inode and \n    the file data remain completely intact because i_nlink is still greater than 0. The data is only wiped from the disk when all hard links are deleted.\n\n    Limitations: Because an inode number is only unique within its own filesystem partition, hard links cannot cross filesystem\n     boundaries (e.g., you cannot hard link a file from an ext4 drive onto a btrfs drive). You also cannot hard link directories (to prevent infinite loops in the filesystem graph).\n\n[dentry: file.txt] ─────► i_ino = 142857 ────► [ struct inode ] ──► Data Blocks\n                               ▲\n[dentry: hardlink.txt] ────────┘ (Points directly to the same Inode)\n\n2. Symbolic Links (Symlinks): A Shortcut File\n\nA symlink is a completely distinct, special type of file that contains a text string pointing to the path of another filename.\n\n    Under the Hood: When you create a symlink, the kernel allocates a brand new inode with its own unique i_ino and a \n    distinct struct inode. The file type bits in i_mode are set to S_IFLNK (Symbolic Link). The data blocks of this new i\n    node simply store the text path string of the target file (e.g., \"/home/user/file.txt\").\n\n    Behavior: When a process tries to open a symlink, the VFS layer reads the path string stored inside the symlink's inode, \n    halts the current lookup, and restarts the path resolution algorithm using the target path. If you delete the original \n    target file, the symlink remains but becomes \"broken\" (pointing to a path that no longer exists).\n\n    Advantages: Because it relies entirely on a text path string, symlinks can span across different physical drives and file \n    systems, and they can point directly to directories without restrictions.\n\n[dentry: symlink.txt] ──► i_ino = 999999 ──► [struct inode (S_IFLNK)] ──► Data: \"file.txt\"\n                                                                                │\n                                                                                ▼\n[dentry: file.txt] ─────► i_ino = 142857 ──────────────────────────────► [struct inode] ──► Data Blocks",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "created": null,
      "labelPosition": null,
      "baseFontSize": null
    },
