        }
      ],
      "updated": 1784470008821,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Why inode stores i_fop as well? (To be explored further)\n\n\nThe short answer: The inode's f_op acts as the master template that dictates how a file descriptor should behave\n when it is first opened.\n\nHere is the exact architectural reason for this design:\n\n1. The Polymorphism Problem: Not All Inodes are Disk Files\n\nIn Linux, an inode doesn't just represent a plain text file on an ext4 drive. An inode can represent a directory, a pipe (FIFO), \na network socket, or a character device (like a mouse or a GPU driver in /dev).\n\nEach of these file types requires radically different operations when you call system calls like read() or write():\n\n    Reading a regular file copies bytes from a storage driver's page cache.\n\n    Reading a network socket pulls packets from a network interface buffer.\n\n    Reading a character device executes custom code inside a hardware driver.\n\nThe kernel needs a way to store which behavior belongs to this entry before anyone even opens it. That is the job of the \ninode's operations pointer.\n\n2. The Initialization Hook (Setting up struct file)\n\nWhen a process calls open(), the kernel sets up a brand new struct file instance. But how does that new struct file know which \nread and write functions to use?\n\nIt copies them directly from the inode. The path looks like this:\n\n// Simplified kernel logic inside the open system call path\nfile->f_op = inode->i_fop; \n\nif (file->f_op->open) {\n    error = file->f_op->open(inode, file); // Trigger custom open routine\n}\n\n3. The Power of Overriding (The Character Device Example)\n\nThe most elegant aspect of this architecture is how the kernel handles hardware drivers via Device Files (like /dev/nvme0).\n\n    When the filesystem mounts /dev, it creates inodes on the disk space. Initially, the generic filesystem driver assigns a \ndefault device-file operation pointer to inode->i_fop.\n\n    When a process opens /dev/nvme0, the generic open function triggers. It looks at the inode's device major/minor numbers, \nrealizes it's a character device, and swaps the generic operations pointer out for the actual custom driver operations written \nby the hardware manufacturer.\n\n    Any subsequent read() or write() calls on that file descriptor instantly route directly into the custom hardware driver code, \ncompletely bypassing the standard filesystem code.",
      "fontSize": 36,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Why inode stores i_fop as well? (To be explored further)\n\n\nThe short answer: The inode's f_op acts as the master template that dictates how a file descriptor should behave\n when it is first opened.\n\nHere is the exact architectural reason for this design:\n\n1. The Polymorphism Problem: Not All Inodes are Disk Files\n\nIn Linux, an inode doesn't just represent a plain text file on an ext4 drive. An inode can represent a directory, a pipe (FIFO), \na network socket, or a character device (like a mouse or a GPU driver in /dev).\n\nEach of these file types requires radically different operations when you call system calls like read() or write():\n\n    Reading a regular file copies bytes from a storage driver's page cache.\n\n    Reading a network socket pulls packets from a network interface buffer.\n\n    Reading a character device executes custom code inside a hardware driver.\n\nThe kernel needs a way to store which behavior belongs to this entry before anyone even opens it. That is the job of the \ninode's operations pointer.\n\n2. The Initialization Hook (Setting up struct file)\n\nWhen a process calls open(), the kernel sets up a brand new struct file instance. But how does that new struct file know which \nread and write functions to use?\n\nIt copies them directly from the inode. The path looks like this:\n\n// Simplified kernel logic inside the open system call path\nfile->f_op = inode->i_fop; \n\nif (file->f_op->open) {\n    error = file->f_op->open(inode, file); // Trigger custom open routine\n}\n\n3. The Power of Overriding (The Character Device Example)\n\nThe most elegant aspect of this architecture is how the kernel handles hardware drivers via Device Files (like /dev/nvme0).\n\n    When the filesystem mounts /dev, it creates inodes on the disk space. Initially, the generic filesystem driver assigns a \ndefault device-file operation pointer to inode->i_fop.\n\n    When a process opens /dev/nvme0, the generic open function triggers. It looks at the inode's device major/minor numbers, \nrealizes it's a character device, and swaps the generic operations pointer out for the actual custom driver operations written \nby the hardware manufacturer.\n\n    Any subsequent read() or write() calls on that file descriptor instantly route directly into the custom hardware driver code, \ncompletely bypassing the standard filesystem code.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "created": null,
      "labelPosition": null,
      "baseFontSize": null
    },
