      "updated": 1784475430579,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "address_space {\n    struct inode *host (Back-pointer to the owning inode. NULL if not inode-backed (e.g. swapper's anonymous pages) \n    struct xarray i_pages (Enhancement of old radix tree page_tree, representation of pages owned by this file)\n\n    unsigned long nrpages (Total pages currently cached for this file)\n    struct rb_root_cached  i_mmap (Represents all userspace processes (VMA's)  that have this page cache mapped into their address space)\n    \n    const struct address_space_operations *a_ops (Operations table for address_space)\n    pgoff_t writeback_index (Where writeback left off — used to resume flushing dirty pages sequentially )\n}",
      "fontSize": 36,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "address_space {\n    struct inode *host (Back-pointer to the owning inode. NULL if not inode-backed (e.g. swapper's anonymous pages) \n    struct xarray i_pages (Enhancement of old radix tree page_tree, representation of pages owned by this file)\n\n    unsigned long nrpages (Total pages currently cached for this file)\n    struct rb_root_cached  i_mmap (Represents all userspace processes (VMA's)  that have this page cache mapped into their address space)\n    \n    const struct address_space_operations *a_ops (Operations table for address_space)\n    pgoff_t writeback_index (Where writeback left off — used to resume flushing dirty pages sequentially )\n}",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "created": null,
      "labelPosition": null,
      "baseFontSize": null
    },
    {
      "id": "N8kcuPVgJQN-PkKVGkFaj",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 39.550184594607344,
      "y": 3367.9237426290033,
      "width": 3826.2666015625,
      "height": 1575,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#2f9e44",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b1M",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 776275761,
      "version": 35,
      "versionNonce": 2065328895,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [
        {
          "id": "vQfdr-2kxEdBjNRa8LqtU",
          "type": "arrow"
        }
      ],
      "updated": 1784475258790,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "i_mmap and i_pages\n\nTo see how i_pages (the XArray) and i_mmap (the cached Red-Black tree) differ, you have to look at the two completely different directions memory flows when a file is memory-mapped (mmap).\n\nBoth live inside struct address_space, but they solve opposite problems. One handles the data, and the other handles the sharing processes.\nThe Short Answer\n\n    i_pages (XArray): Maps a file offset to the physical memory pages (Folios) containing the data. It asks: \"Where is the actual data for page index 5 in RAM?\"\n\n    i_mmap (RB-Tree): Maps the file to all the userspace processes (vm_area_structs) that currently have this file mapped into their virtual memory. It asks: \"Which processes are currently looking at this file?\"\n\nWhy i_mmap Needs a Red-Black Tree\n\nWhen you memory-map a file, you aren't loading the whole file into a process's memory at once. Instead, the kernel creates a vm_area_struct (VMA) in your process that points to a specific range of the file.\n\nIf ten different processes map different parts of the same massive database file, the kernel needs a fast way to track all those different processes and their overlapping view windows. That is what i_mmap does. \nIt uses an augmented Red-Black tree (rb_root_cached) to store intervals of VMAs sorted by their file offsets.\n\nThe Dynamic Interaction (When they meet)\n\nThe easiest way to understand the difference is to trace what happens during a Page Fault or a Cache Eviction. This is where the two structures talk to each other.\nScenario A: Reading/Writing Data (Using i_pages)\n\nWhen a process reads from a memory-mapped file address and the data isn't in RAM yet, a page fault triggers. The kernel looks inside i_pages (XArray) using the file offset. If it's a miss, it reads the data from disk, \nallocates a physical page, and drops it into i_pages.\nScenario B: Unmapping or Evicting (Using i_mmap)\n\nWhat happens if the system runs out of memory, and the kernel needs to evict a dirty file page from RAM? Or what if a process calls msync() to flush changes back to disk?\nThe kernel has the physical page, but it needs to find every single process table entry pointing to it to clear the \"Present\" bit.\n\n    The kernel looks at the page and finds its address_space.\n\n    It looks at i_mmap (RB-Tree) to search for all process VMAs that cover this specific file offset.\n\n    It walks down those specific VMAs, unmaps the page from those processes' page tables, and then safely frees the physical page from i_pages.",
      "fontSize": 36,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "i_mmap and i_pages\n\nTo see how i_pages (the XArray) and i_mmap (the cached Red-Black tree) differ, you have to look at the two completely different directions memory flows when a file is memory-mapped (mmap).\n\nBoth live inside struct address_space, but they solve opposite problems. One handles the data, and the other handles the sharing processes.\nThe Short Answer\n\n    i_pages (XArray): Maps a file offset to the physical memory pages (Folios) containing the data. It asks: \"Where is the actual data for page index 5 in RAM?\"\n\n    i_mmap (RB-Tree): Maps the file to all the userspace processes (vm_area_structs) that currently have this file mapped into their virtual memory. It asks: \"Which processes are currently looking at this file?\"\n\nWhy i_mmap Needs a Red-Black Tree\n\nWhen you memory-map a file, you aren't loading the whole file into a process's memory at once. Instead, the kernel creates a vm_area_struct (VMA) in your process that points to a specific range of the file.\n\nIf ten different processes map different parts of the same massive database file, the kernel needs a fast way to track all those different processes and their overlapping view windows. That is what i_mmap does. \nIt uses an augmented Red-Black tree (rb_root_cached) to store intervals of VMAs sorted by their file offsets.\n\nThe Dynamic Interaction (When they meet)\n\nThe easiest way to understand the difference is to trace what happens during a Page Fault or a Cache Eviction. This is where the two structures talk to each other.\nScenario A: Reading/Writing Data (Using i_pages)\n\nWhen a process reads from a memory-mapped file address and the data isn't in RAM yet, a page fault triggers. The kernel looks inside i_pages (XArray) using the file offset. If it's a miss, it reads the data from disk, \nallocates a physical page, and drops it into i_pages.\nScenario B: Unmapping or Evicting (Using i_mmap)\n\nWhat happens if the system runs out of memory, and the kernel needs to evict a dirty file page from RAM? Or what if a process calls msync() to flush changes back to disk?\nThe kernel has the physical page, but it needs to find every single process table entry pointing to it to clear the \"Present\" bit.\n\n    The kernel looks at the page and finds its address_space.\n\n    It looks at i_mmap (RB-Tree) to search for all process VMAs that cover this specific file offset.\n\n    It walks down those specific VMAs, unmaps the page from those processes' page tables, and then safely frees the physical page from i_pages.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "created": null,
      "labelPosition": null,
      "baseFontSize": null
    },
